6.3 The Coefficient of Determination (\(R^2\))
The coefficient of determination or \(R^2\) measures the percent of variation in \(y\) explained by variations in \(x\). It is calculated by:

THE COEFFICIENT OF DETERMINATION \[R^2 = r^2\] where \(r\) is the correlation coefficient. \(R^2\) ranges from \(0\) (no relationship) to \(1\) (perfect relationship).


Example: Consider once more the SPY and BND example. The correlation coefficient is \(r = -0.89\). Hence:
\[R^2 = (-0.89)^2 = 0.79\]
This indicates that about \(79\%\) of the variation in BND can be explained by changes in SPY. The two variables are closely related.



Try it Correlation explorer

Drag the slider to set how strongly ad spend and sales move together. Then add an outlier and see how a single point can change \(r\) and \(R^2\).

viewof rTarget = Inputs.range([-1, 1], {value: 0.7, step: 0.05, label: "Target correlation"})
viewof rN = Inputs.range([10, 200], {value: 50, step: 5, label: "Number of stores"})
viewof rOut = Inputs.toggle({label: "Add an outlier", value: false})
viewof rGo = Inputs.button("New sample")






























rBase = {
  rGo;
  const g = d3.randomNormal.source(d3.randomLcg(Math.random()))(0, 1);
  return d3.range(rN).map(() =&gt; [g(), g()]);
}
rPts = {
  const pts = rBase.map(([z1, z2]) =&gt; {
    const zy = rTarget * z1 + Math.sqrt(Math.max(0, 1 - rTarget**2)) * z2;
    return {x: 50 + 15 * z1, y: 200 + 40 * zy, out: false};
  });
  if (rOut) pts.push({x: 105, y: rTarget &gt;= 0 ? 90 : 330, out: true});
  return pts;
}
rStats = {
  const n = rPts.length, mx = d3.mean(rPts, d =&gt; d.x), my = d3.mean(rPts, d =&gt; d.y);
  const sxy = d3.sum(rPts, d =&gt; (d.x - mx) * (d.y - my)) / (n - 1);
  const sx = d3.deviation(rPts, d =&gt; d.x), sy = d3.deviation(rPts, d =&gt; d.y);
  const r = sxy / (sx * sy), b1 = sxy / (sx * sx), b0 = my - b1 * mx;
  return {sxy, r, r2: r * r, b0, b1};
}
Plot.plot({
  height: 300, marginLeft: 44, marginBottom: 40,
  style: {fontFamily: "Inter, sans-serif", fontSize: "12px", background: "transparent", color: "var(--bs-ink-2)"},
  x: {label: "Ad spend ($000s) →", domain: [0, 115]},
  y: {label: "↑ Sales ($000s)", domain: [40, 360], grid: true},
  marks: [
    Plot.dot(rPts.filter(d =&gt; !d.out), {x: "x", y: "y", r: 3.5, fill: "var(--bs-part)", fillOpacity: 0.65}),
    Plot.dot(rPts.filter(d =&gt; d.out), {x: "x", y: "y", r: 7, stroke: "var(--bs-plot-2)", strokeWidth: 2.5}),
    Plot.line([[0, rStats.b0], [115, rStats.b0 + 115 * rStats.b1]], {stroke: "var(--bs-ink)", strokeWidth: 1.5})
  ]
})






























html`&lt;div class="bs-stats"&gt;
  &lt;div class="bs-stat"&gt;&lt;div class="k"&gt;Covariance s&lt;sub&gt;xy&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="v"&gt;${rStats.sxy.toFixed(1)}&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="bs-stat hl"&gt;&lt;div class="k"&gt;Correlation r&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="v"&gt;${rStats.r.toFixed(2)}&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="bs-stat"&gt;&lt;div class="k"&gt;R²&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="v"&gt;${(rStats.r2*100).toFixed(0)}%&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="bs-stat"&gt;&lt;div class="k"&gt;Strength&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="v" style="font-size:1.05rem;padding-top:.35rem"&gt;${Math.abs(rStats.r) &gt;= 0.7 ? "Strong" : Math.abs(rStats.r) &gt;= 0.3 ? "Moderate" : "Weak"} ${rStats.r &gt;= 0 ? "direct" : "inverse"}&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="bs-excel"&gt;&lt;span class="lbl"&gt;Excel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;=COVARIANCE.S(A2:A${rPts.length+1}, B2:B${rPts.length+1})&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;=CORREL(A2:A${rPts.length+1}, B2:B${rPts.length+1})&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;=CORREL(A2:A${rPts.length+1}, B2:B${rPts.length+1})^2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;`

9.3 Discrete Uniform Distribution
The discrete uniform distribution is a probability distribution that assigns equal probability to each outcome in a finite set of possible outcomes. In other words, each outcome in the set is equally likely to occur.

THE DISCRETE UNIFORM DISTRIBUTION \[f(x)=1/n\] \[E(x)=\frac {\sum x_i}{n}\] \[var(x)=\frac {\sum (x_i-E(x))^2}{n}\] where \(n\) is the number of possible outcomes and \(x_i\) are the possible values.


Example: Consider tossing a fair die. Since all outcomes are equally likely, the probability of the die landing on 6 is \(f(x)=1/6\). The expected value on a roll of a die is \(E(x)=\frac {1+2+3+4+5+6}{6}=3.5\) and the variance is \(var(x)=\frac {(-2.5)^2+(-1.5)^2+(-0.5)^2+0.5^2+1.5^2+2.5^2}{6}\approx 2.92\).



Try it Discrete uniform explorer

Every value from \(a\) to \(b\) is equally likely, so every bar has the same height, \(1/n\). Widen the range and watch each bar shrink while the mean stays in the middle. The defaults are a fair die.

viewof duA = Inputs.range([0, 10], {value: 1, step: 1, label: "Smallest value a"})
viewof duB = Inputs.range([duA + 1, duA + 20], {value: Math.max(duA + 1, Math.min(6, duA + 20)), step: 1, label: "Largest value b"})
viewof duX = Inputs.range([duA, duB], {value: Math.min(duB, Math.max(duA, 3)), step: 1, label: "Value x"})























duComb = {
  const lf = k =&gt; { let s = 0; for (let i = 2; i &lt;= k; i++) s += Math.log(i); return s; };
  const lnC = (n, k) =&gt; (k &lt; 0 || k &gt; n) ? -Infinity : lf(n) - lf(k) - lf(n - k);
  return {lf, lnC, C: (n, k) =&gt; (k &lt; 0 || k &gt; n) ? 0 : Math.round(Math.exp(lnC(n, k)))};
}
duNum = v =&gt; Number.isInteger(v) ? v : +v.toFixed(4)
duBig = v =&gt; (Number.isInteger(v) && v &lt; 1e12) ? v.toLocaleString() : (v &gt;= 1e-4 && v &lt; 1e6 ? v.toPrecision(4) : v.toExponential(3).replace(/e([+-])(\d+)/, (m, sg, d) =&gt; ` × 10^${sg === '-' ? '−' : ''}${d}`))
duxs = d3.range(duA, duB + 1)
dun = duxs.length
duf = x =&gt; (x &gt;= duA && x &lt;= duB) ? 1 / dun : 0
duExtra = null
duPlugF = `1/n = 1/${dun} = ${(1 / dun).toFixed(4)}`
duPlugE = `(${dun &lt;= 8 ? duxs.join(" + ") : duxs.slice(0, 3).join(" + ") + " + … + " + duB}) / ${dun} = ${d3.mean(duxs).toFixed(2)}`
duPlugV = `Σ(xᵢ − ${d3.mean(duxs).toFixed(2)})² / ${dun} = ${(d3.sum(duxs, x =&gt; (x - d3.mean(duxs)) ** 2) / dun).toFixed(2)}`
duExcelF = `f(x) =1/${dun}`
duExcelC = `P(X ≤ ${duX}) =(${duX}-${duA}+1)/${dun}`






















































































duData = duxs.map(x =&gt; ({x, p: duf(x)}))
duCum = d3.sum(duData.filter(d =&gt; d.x &lt;= duX), d =&gt; d.p)
duFx = duf(duX)
duMean = d3.sum(duData, d =&gt; d.x * d.p)
duVar = d3.sum(duData, d =&gt; (d.x - duMean) ** 2 * d.p)
duFmt = v =&gt; v &lt; 0.0001 && v &gt; 0 ? v.toExponential(2) : v.toFixed(4)
duAllX = Array.from(new Set([...duxs, ...(duExtra ? duExtra.map(d =&gt; d.x) : [])])).sort((a, b) =&gt; a - b)
duYmax = Math.max(d3.max(duData, d =&gt; d.p), duExtra ? d3.max(duExtra, d =&gt; d.p) : 0) * 1.22
Plot.plot({
  width: Math.max(300, width - 50), height: 250, marginLeft: 42, marginRight: 10, marginBottom: 38,
  style: {fontFamily: "Inter, sans-serif", fontSize: "12px", background: "transparent", color: "var(--bs-ink-2)"},
  x: {label: "Value x →", labelAnchor: "right", domain: [d3.min(duAllX) - 0.6, d3.max(duAllX) + 0.6],
      ticks: duAllX.length &lt;= 26 ? duAllX : undefined, tickFormat: d =&gt; Number.isInteger(d) ? d : ""},
  y: {label: "↑ f(x)", domain: [0, duYmax], grid: true, tickFormat: d =&gt; d.toFixed(2)},
  marks: [
    Plot.rectY(duData, {x1: d =&gt; d.x - 0.4, x2: d =&gt; d.x + 0.4, y: "p", fill: "var(--bs-part)",
      fillOpacity: d =&gt; d.x === duX ? 1 : d.x &lt; duX ? 0.45 : 0.18}),
    ...(duExtra ? [Plot.dot(duExtra, {x: "x", y: "p", r: 3.5, fill: "var(--bs-ink)"}),
                    Plot.line(duExtra, {x: "x", y: "p", stroke: "var(--bs-ink)", strokeWidth: 1, strokeDasharray: "3,3"})] : []),
    Plot.ruleX([duMean], {stroke: "var(--bs-ink)", strokeWidth: 1.5, strokeDasharray: "4,3"}),
    Plot.text([duMean], {x: d =&gt; d, frameAnchor: "top", dy: 2, dx: 5, textAnchor: "start", text: d =&gt; "E(x) = " + d.toFixed(2), fill: "var(--bs-ink)", fontWeight: 600}),
    Plot.text([duX], {x: d =&gt; d, y: duFx, dy: -8, text: () =&gt; duFmt(duFx), fill: "var(--bs-part)", fontWeight: 600}),
    Plot.ruleY([0], {stroke: "var(--bs-line)"})
  ]
})

































































html`&lt;div style="display:flex;gap:.9rem;flex-wrap:wrap;font-size:.82rem;color:var(--bs-ink-2);margin:.1rem 0 .6rem"&gt;
  &lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="display:inline-block;width:11px;height:11px;border-radius:3px;background:var(--bs-part);vertical-align:-1px"&gt;&lt;/span&gt; f(${duX}) = P(X = ${duX})&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="display:inline-block;width:11px;height:11px;border-radius:3px;background:var(--bs-part);opacity:.45;vertical-align:-1px"&gt;&lt;/span&gt; light + dark bars together: P(X ≤ ${duX})&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="bs-stats"&gt;
  &lt;div class="bs-stat hl"&gt;&lt;div class="k"&gt;f(${duX}) = P(X = ${duX})&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="v"&gt;${duFmt(duFx)}&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="bs-stat"&gt;&lt;div class="k"&gt;P(X ≤ ${duX})&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="v"&gt;${Math.min(1, duCum).toFixed(4)}&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="bs-stat"&gt;&lt;div class="k"&gt;E(x)&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="v"&gt;${duMean.toFixed(2)}&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="bs-stat"&gt;&lt;div class="k"&gt;var(x)&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="v"&gt;${duVar.toFixed(2)}&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="bs-formula-live"&gt;
  &lt;div&gt;&lt;span class="lbl"&gt;f(x)&lt;/span&gt; ${duPlugF}&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div&gt;&lt;span class="lbl"&gt;E(x)&lt;/span&gt; ${duPlugE}&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div&gt;&lt;span class="lbl"&gt;var(x)&lt;/span&gt; ${duPlugV}&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="bs-excel"&gt;&lt;span class="lbl"&gt;Excel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;${duExcelF}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;${duExcelC}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;`

9.4 Binomial Distribution
The binomial distribution is a probability distribution that describes the outcome of a sequence of \(n\) independent Bernoulli trials. In a Bernoulli trial, there are only two possible outcomes: “success” and “failure”. The probability of success is denoted by \(p\), and the probability of failure is denoted by \(q = 1 - p\). In a sequence of \(n\) independent Bernoulli trials, the number of successes (\(x\)) is a random variable that follows a binomial distribution.

THE BINOMIAL DISTRIBUTION \[f(x)=C_x^n (p^x)(1-p)^{n-x}\] \[E(x)=np \qquad var(x)=np(1-p)\] where \(n\) is the number of trials, \(x\) is the number of successes, \(p\) is the probability of success, and \(C_x^n\) is the number of ways there can be \(x\) successes in \(n\) trials.


Example: Consider the following scenario. Imagine your favorite team in the NBA Finals, trailing by 2 points with seconds left. Your star player, a 90% free-throw shooter, gets three shots. What’s the chance your team takes the lead?
Since shooting free-throws can be thought as a binomial experiment, we can find the probability using the binomial probability mass function. To take the lead, the star player needs to make all three shots. We can substitute three into the function:
\[f(3)=C_3^3 (0.9^3)(0.1)^{0}=0.9^3=0.729\]
The function indicates that there is a 72.9% chance of taking the lead.



Try it Binomial explorer

Count the successes in \(n\) independent trials, each with success probability \(p\). Move \(p\) toward \(0.5\) and the shape turns symmetric; push it toward \(0\) or \(1\) and it piles up on one side. The defaults are the three free throws by a 90% shooter.

viewof biN = Inputs.range([1, 40], {value: 3, step: 1, label: "Trials n"})
viewof biP = Inputs.range([0.01, 0.99], {value: 0.9, step: 0.01, label: "Success probability p"})
viewof biX = Inputs.range([0, biN], {value: Math.min(biN, 3), step: 1, label: "Successes x"})























biComb = {
  const lf = k =&gt; { let s = 0; for (let i = 2; i &lt;= k; i++) s += Math.log(i); return s; };
  const lnC = (n, k) =&gt; (k &lt; 0 || k &gt; n) ? -Infinity : lf(n) - lf(k) - lf(n - k);
  return {lf, lnC, C: (n, k) =&gt; (k &lt; 0 || k &gt; n) ? 0 : Math.round(Math.exp(lnC(n, k)))};
}
biNum = v =&gt; Number.isInteger(v) ? v : +v.toFixed(4)
biBig = v =&gt; (Number.isInteger(v) && v &lt; 1e12) ? v.toLocaleString() : (v &gt;= 1e-4 && v &lt; 1e6 ? v.toPrecision(4) : v.toExponential(3).replace(/e([+-])(\d+)/, (m, sg, d) =&gt; ` × 10^${sg === '-' ? '−' : ''}${d}`))
bixs = d3.range(0, biN + 1)
bif = x =&gt; Math.exp(biComb.lnC(biN, x) + x * Math.log(biP) + (biN - x) * Math.log(1 - biP))
biExtra = null
biPlugF = html`C&lt;sup&gt;${biN}&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;sub style="margin-left:-.5em"&gt;${biX}&lt;/sub&gt; · ${biP}&lt;sup&gt;${biX}&lt;/sup&gt; · ${biNum(1 - biP)}&lt;sup&gt;${biN - biX}&lt;/sup&gt; = ${biComb.C(biN, biX).toLocaleString()} × ${Math.pow(biP, biX).toPrecision(4)} × ${Math.pow(1 - biP, biN - biX).toPrecision(4)} = ${bif(biX).toFixed(4)}`
biPlugE = `np = ${biN} × ${biP} = ${(biN * biP).toFixed(2)}`
biPlugV = `np(1 − p) = ${biN} × ${biP} × ${biNum(1 - biP)} = ${(biN * biP * (1 - biP)).toFixed(2)}`
biExcelF = `=BINOM.DIST(${biX}, ${biN}, ${biP}, FALSE)`
biExcelC = `=BINOM.DIST(${biX}, ${biN}, ${biP}, TRUE)`















































































biData = bixs.map(x =&gt; ({x, p: bif(x)}))
biCum = d3.sum(biData.filter(d =&gt; d.x &lt;= biX), d =&gt; d.p)
biFx = bif(biX)
biMean = d3.sum(biData, d =&gt; d.x * d.p)
biVar = d3.sum(biData, d =&gt; (d.x - biMean) ** 2 * d.p)
biFmt = v =&gt; v &lt; 0.0001 && v &gt; 0 ? v.toExponential(2) : v.toFixed(4)
biAllX = Array.from(new Set([...bixs, ...(biExtra ? biExtra.map(d =&gt; d.x) : [])])).sort((a, b) =&gt; a - b)
biYmax = Math.max(d3.max(biData, d =&gt; d.p), biExtra ? d3.max(biExtra, d =&gt; d.p) : 0) * 1.22
Plot.plot({
  width: Math.max(300, width - 50), height: 250, marginLeft: 42, marginRight: 10, marginBottom: 38,
  style: {fontFamily: "Inter, sans-serif", fontSize: "12px", background: "transparent", color: "var(--bs-ink-2)"},
  x: {label: "Number of successes x →", labelAnchor: "right", domain: [d3.min(biAllX) - 0.6, d3.max(biAllX) + 0.6],
      ticks: biAllX.length &lt;= 26 ? biAllX : undefined, tickFormat: d =&gt; Number.isInteger(d) ? d : ""},
  y: {label: "↑ f(x)", domain: [0, biYmax], grid: true, tickFormat: d =&gt; d.toFixed(2)},
  marks: [
    Plot.rectY(biData, {x1: d =&gt; d.x - 0.4, x2: d =&gt; d.x + 0.4, y: "p", fill: "var(--bs-part)",
      fillOpacity: d =&gt; d.x === biX ? 1 : d.x &lt; biX ? 0.45 : 0.18}),
    ...(biExtra ? [Plot.dot(biExtra, {x: "x", y: "p", r: 3.5, fill: "var(--bs-ink)"}),
                    Plot.line(biExtra, {x: "x", y: "p", stroke: "var(--bs-ink)", strokeWidth: 1, strokeDasharray: "3,3"})] : []),
    Plot.ruleX([biMean], {stroke: "var(--bs-ink)", strokeWidth: 1.5, strokeDasharray: "4,3"}),
    Plot.text([biMean], {x: d =&gt; d, frameAnchor: "top", dy: 2, dx: 5, textAnchor: "start", text: d =&gt; "E(x) = " + d.toFixed(2), fill: "var(--bs-ink)", fontWeight: 600}),
    Plot.text([biX], {x: d =&gt; d, y: biFx, dy: -8, text: () =&gt; biFmt(biFx), fill: "var(--bs-part)", fontWeight: 600}),
    Plot.ruleY([0], {stroke: "var(--bs-line)"})
  ]
})

































































html`&lt;div style="display:flex;gap:.9rem;flex-wrap:wrap;font-size:.82rem;color:var(--bs-ink-2);margin:.1rem 0 .6rem"&gt;
  &lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="display:inline-block;width:11px;height:11px;border-radius:3px;background:var(--bs-part);vertical-align:-1px"&gt;&lt;/span&gt; f(${biX}) = P(X = ${biX})&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="display:inline-block;width:11px;height:11px;border-radius:3px;background:var(--bs-part);opacity:.45;vertical-align:-1px"&gt;&lt;/span&gt; light + dark bars together: P(X ≤ ${biX})&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="bs-stats"&gt;
  &lt;div class="bs-stat hl"&gt;&lt;div class="k"&gt;f(${biX}) = P(X = ${biX})&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="v"&gt;${biFmt(biFx)}&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="bs-stat"&gt;&lt;div class="k"&gt;P(X ≤ ${biX})&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="v"&gt;${Math.min(1, biCum).toFixed(4)}&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="bs-stat"&gt;&lt;div class="k"&gt;E(x)&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="v"&gt;${biMean.toFixed(2)}&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="bs-stat"&gt;&lt;div class="k"&gt;var(x)&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="v"&gt;${biVar.toFixed(2)}&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="bs-formula-live"&gt;
  &lt;div&gt;&lt;span class="lbl"&gt;f(x)&lt;/span&gt; ${biPlugF}&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div&gt;&lt;span class="lbl"&gt;E(x)&lt;/span&gt; ${biPlugE}&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div&gt;&lt;span class="lbl"&gt;var(x)&lt;/span&gt; ${biPlugV}&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="bs-excel"&gt;&lt;span class="lbl"&gt;Excel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;${biExcelF}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;${biExcelC}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;`

9.5 The Hypergeometric Distribution
The hypergeometric distribution is a probability distribution that describes the outcome of drawing a sample from a population without replacement. It is used to calculate the probability of drawing a certain number of successes (\(x\)) in a sample of a given size (\(n\)), where the success or failure of each individual draw is dependent on the success or failure of other draws.
The hypergeometric experiment differs from the binomial since:

trials are not independent.
the probability of success changes from trial to trial.


THE HYPERGEOMETRIC DISTRIBUTION \[f(x)=\frac {C_x^r C_{n-x}^{N-r}}{C_n^N}\] \[E(x)=n \frac {r}{N} \qquad var(x)= n \frac {r}{N} \left(1- \frac {r}{N}\right) \left(\frac {N-n}{N-1}\right)\] where \(n\) is the sample size, \(x\) is the number of successes in the sample, \(r\) is the number of successes in the population, and \(N\) is the number of elements in the population.


Example: Picture a box of 12 electric fuses from the manufacturer Ontario Electric. Five fuses are known to be defective. An inspector randomly picks 3 fuses to test. What is the probability that exactly one of the three chosen is defective?
We can use the hypergeometric probability mass function to solve this problem. From the problem we know that \(N=12\), \(n=3\), \(r=5\), and \(x=1\). Substituting these values into the PMF yields:
\[f(1)=\frac {C_1^5 C_{4}^{7}}{C_3^{12}}=0.4772\]
There is a 47.72% chance that the inspector finds a defective fuse in his sample of three.



Try it Hypergeometric explorer

Draw \(n\) items without replacement from a population of \(N\) that contains \(r\) successes. Turn on the comparison to see the binomial with the same success rate \(r/N\): the two agree when the sample is a small slice of the population and drift apart as \(n\) approaches \(N\). The defaults are the box of 12 fuses with 5 defective.

viewof hgNN = Inputs.range([2, 60], {value: 12, step: 1, label: "Population N"})
viewof hgR = Inputs.range([0, hgNN], {value: Math.min(hgNN, 5), step: 1, label: "Successes in population r"})
viewof hgN = Inputs.range([1, hgNN], {value: Math.min(hgNN, 3), step: 1, label: "Sample size n"})
viewof hgX = Inputs.range([Math.max(0, hgN - (hgNN - hgR)), Math.min(hgN, hgR)], {value: Math.max(Math.max(0, hgN - (hgNN - hgR)), Math.min(1, hgN, hgR)), step: 1, label: "Successes in sample x"})
viewof hgCmp = Inputs.toggle({label: "Compare with binomial", value: false})





































hgComb = {
  const lf = k =&gt; { let s = 0; for (let i = 2; i &lt;= k; i++) s += Math.log(i); return s; };
